3. THE DAILY TEAM WORKFLOW (BRANCHING & COMMITTING)
================================================================================
NEVER work directly on 'main'. Always create a feature branch for your work.

--- Step 1: Switch to main and update your local code ---
git checkout main
git pull origin main

--- Step 2: Create and switch to a new feature branch ---
# Standard naming convention: feature/description, fix/bug-description
git checkout -b feature/add-login-page

--- Step 3: Work on your code and check status ---
# See which files were modified, created, or deleted
git status

# See detailed line-by-line changes made so far
git diff

--- Step 4: Stage your changes ---
# Stage a specific file
git add filename.js

# OR stage all modified files in current folder
git add .

--- Step 5: Commit your changes ---
# Write clear, descriptive commit messages
git commit -m "feat: implement Google login button"

--- Step 6: Push your feature branch to GitHub ---
git push -u origin feature/add-login-page


================================================================================
4. PULL REQUESTS & SYNCING WITH TEAM CODE
================================================================================

--- Step 1: Open a Pull Request on GitHub ---
1. Go to your GitHub repository page in the browser.
2. Click "Compare & pull request" next to your pushed branch.
3. Add a detailed description of your changes and tag teammates for review.
4. Click "Create Pull Request".

--- Step 2: Sync your local feature branch with team updates ---
If teammates merged code into 'main' while you were working on your branch,
bring their changes into your branch before finishing your PR:

# Fetch updated main from GitHub
git fetch origin

# Option A: Merge main into your branch (Standard)
git merge origin/main

# Option B: Rebase your commits on top of main (Cleaner history)
git rebase origin/main


